{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/how-soap-removes-grease/audio/main-b91726887bff.mp3","durationSec":104.76,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.48,"text":"Water is very good at cleaning many things."},{"start":3,"end":4.96,"text":"But water and oil do not mix."},{"start":5.88,"end":9.04,"text":"When you pour oil into water, the oil floats on top."},{"start":9.44,"end":10.98,"text":"It does not go into the water."},{"start":11.72,"end":15.84,"text":"This means if you rub greasy hands with only water, the grease stays on your skin."},{"start":16.28,"end":17.9,"text":"The water just moves around it."},{"start":18.9,"end":23.4,"text":"According to the CDC, washing with only water is less effective than washing with soap."},{"start":23.4,"end":27.32,"text":"A soap molecule is very small — too small to see."},{"start":28.04,"end":30.26,"text":"But it has two ends that do different jobs."},{"start":30.84,"end":31.86,"text":"One end loves water."},{"start":32.28,"end":34.08,"text":"The other end loves oil and fat."},{"start":34.9,"end":38.84,"text":"Because of this, soap can connect to both water and oil at the same time."},{"start":39.54,"end":43.32,"text":"Think of soap as a tiny bridge between two things that do not usually mix."},{"start":43.94,"end":48.36,"text":"The American Chemical Society explains that this is why soap works where water alone cannot."},{"start":49.17,"end":53.58,"text":"When you lather soap on your hands, many soap molecules move toward the grease."},{"start":54.04,"end":56.5,"text":"Their oil-loving ends grab onto the grease."},{"start":57.14,"end":61.32,"text":"At the same time, their water-loving ends point outward toward the water."},{"start":62.26,"end":65.96,"text":"The soap molecules form a tiny round group called a micelle."},{"start":66.5,"end":68.44,"text":"The grease is trapped inside the micelle."},{"start":69.26,"end":76.54,"text":"According to the American Chemical Society, these micelles let water carry the trapped grease away when you rinse."},{"start":77.7,"end":81.2,"text":"Rubbing your hands together with soap is very important."},{"start":82.14,"end":87.68,"text":"Rubbing helps the soap molecules reach all parts of your hands — between your fingers and under your nails."},{"start":88.48,"end":90.96,"text":"Lathering also helps lift germs and chemicals off the skin."},{"start":91.6,"end":101.66,"text":"The CDC says that lather and rubbing help remove germs and chemicals, and that very hot water is not needed."},{"start":101.66,"end":104.38,"text":"Cool or warm water works well."}],"words":[{"text":"Water","start":0,"end":0.38},{"text":"is","start":0.38,"end":0.72},{"text":"very","start":0.72,"end":1.04},{"text":"good","start":1.04,"end":1.24},{"text":"at","start":1.24,"end":1.42},{"text":"cleaning","start":1.42,"end":1.64},{"text":"many","start":1.64,"end":1.94},{"text":"things","start":1.94,"end":2.48},{"text":"But","start":3,"end":3.22},{"text":"water","start":3.22,"end":3.54},{"text":"and","start":3.54,"end":3.82},{"text":"oil","start":3.82,"end":4.12},{"text":"do","start":4.12,"end":4.36},{"text":"not","start":4.36,"end":4.62},{"text":"mix","start":4.62,"end":4.96},{"text":"When","start":5.88,"end":6},{"text":"you","start":6,"end":6.18},{"text":"pour","start":6.18,"end":6.44},{"text":"oil","start":6.44,"end":6.72},{"text":"into","start":6.72,"end":7.02},{"text":"water","start":7.02,"end":7.38},{"text":"the","start":7.76,"end":7.86},{"text":"oil","start":7.86,"end":8.12},{"text":"floats","start":8.12,"end":8.46},{"text":"on","start":8.46,"end":8.74},{"text":"top","start":8.74,"end":9.04},{"text":"It","start":9.44,"end":9.52},{"text":"does","start":9.52,"end":9.7},{"text":"not","start":9.7,"end":10.04},{"text":"go","start":10.04,"end":10.3},{"text":"into","start":10.3,"end":10.52},{"text":"the","start":10.52,"end":10.7},{"text":"water","start":10.7,"end":10.98},{"text":"This","start":11.72,"end":11.96},{"text":"means","start":11.96,"end":12.22},{"text":